23.4  sed

TL;DR
  - sed edits text line by line as it flows through. Its most used command is
    s/old/new/.
  - Without g, only the first match on each line changes; s/old/new/g changes
    them all.
  - -i edits the file in place. GNU sed (Linux) takes -i alone; macOS needs
    -i ''.

      sed 's/http:/https:/g' links.txt
      sed -i.bak 's/DEBUG=1/DEBUG=0/' .env    # keeps a copy in .env.bak
      sed -n '10,20p' big.log                 # print lines 10 to 20

Pitfalls
  - Run the command without -i first and read the output: -i has no undo, unless
    you give it a suffix like -i.bak.
  - When the text contains /, use another separator: s|/usr/local|/opt|.
  - In the replacement, & means "what matched": write \& for a literal
    ampersand.
  - As with grep, + and | need -E to have their usual meaning.
  - sed -i on a symbolic link replaces the link with a regular file, unless you
    add --follow-symlinks.

Deep dive

  ▾ How does sed work?

    It reads one line, runs the script on it, prints the result, then moves to
    the next line. -n turns off the automatic printing, so only p prints.

    A command can be limited to some lines with an address in front: /^#/d
    deletes comment lines, 5,10s/a/b/ only replaces on lines 5 to 10, and
    /start/,/end/ covers everything between two matches.

  ▾ How do I reuse part of the match?

    With -E, parentheses capture, and \1, \2 bring the captures back in the
    replacement. This swaps "user@host" into "host: user":

        sed -E 's/([a-z]+)@([a-z.]+)/\2: \1/' contacts.txt

